## Bottom line

1. **Industry payments and brand-name prescribing.** After comparing each prescriber only
   with unpaid peers in the same specialty and state, industry-paid prescribers wrote
   **{{ gap | abs_pct }} {{ 'more' if gap >= 0 else 'fewer' }} brand-name fills** than expected.
   {% if reg is not none %}
   A regression that also accounts for patient mix and prescribing volume puts the
   difference at **{{ reg | signed }}**.
   {% endif %}
2. **Potential savings.** Bringing paid prescribers' brand use down to their unpaid peers'
   level, on drugs where a generic version was dispensed, is worth about
   **{{ m.savings.estimated_savings_usd | money }} a year** in gross Part D drug spending,
   before manufacturer rebates. With brand rebates and only partial switching it falls to as
   little as {{ (conservative | map(attribute='estimated_savings_usd') | min) | money }}.
3. **An early-warning score, tested on exclusions it never saw.** The score's weights were
   learned from exclusions before {{ m.outlier_score.design.split_date }}. On exclusions after that
   date, the 1% of prescribers it ranked highest accounted for **{{ lift | lift }}** their
   expected share ({{ m.outlier_score.events_captured }} of {{ m.outlier_score.events_total }}).
   {% if lift is not none and lift <= 1 %}
   That is no better than chance, so the score should not be used for targeting as is.
   {% endif %}

